# Native restic v2 compression

New snapshots in version 2 repositories write authenticated compressed data/tree records and compressed snapshot/index metadata. Version 1 writes stay uncompressed. The interface offers `auto`, `off`, `fastest`, `better`, and `max`; `off` applies only to file data. Existing data is authenticated and reused, so switching modes does not recompress earlier snapshots.

The format contracts were studied from the pinned [restic repository implementation](https://github.com/restic/restic/blob/6aa3a516ce654808a1f28f9fa21e9b7c8e6e90bf/internal/repository/repository.go). This is source-assisted engineering. The [preserved source and review manifest](results/compression-source-study/preservation-manifest.json) binds the original source, BSD license, exact staged implementations, original tests, and independent review. Those records describe their historical runs; their source bytes have been preserved rather than rebound to current code.

The writer uses Node/libzstd levels 1, 3, 7, and 19 with a 512 KiB window and no frame checksum. These settings are a separate policy from the original Go encoder; equal output bytes, equal compression levels, and improved speed or resource use have not been established. Each encoded frame is independently decoded and compared to the plaintext before writing. Input is bounded to 16 MiB per encoded blob/document, and output allocation has an explicit bound. Synchronous native encoding has no hard time preemption or isolated process-memory guarantee.

The [current validation](results/compression-validation.json) covers six writer configurations: version 1 plus every version 2 mode. The authenticated original restic 0.19.1 executable inspects their indexes, performs whole-repository `check --read-data`, and restores deterministic binary, empty, Unicode, and compressible files byte for byte. Independent cases cover mixed legacy plaintext/compressed repositories, appending to an original-created repository, deduplication across mode changes, exact input/window bounds, MAC/frame/index corruption, and refusal before restore output.

One acceptance difference is explicit: the original accepts a valid snapshot with 17 MiB of trailing JSON whitespace; this application's decoded metadata limit is 16 MiB, so it refuses that repository record. Source capture still uses fixed 1 MiB chunks and one blob per pack. Whole-repository checking, Rabin boundaries, batching, remote storage, complete metadata restoration, cancellation, and many other restic features remain incomplete. No full feature parity, cryptographic security equivalence, or universal superiority is established.
